       proxy                  range   slope d log a0/d proxy  bootstrap err  sigma from zero
        type           0.00 - 11.00                  -0.0229         0.0097             -2.4
        fbul            0.00 - 0.84                   0.1340         0.1456              0.9
        fgas            0.02 - 0.95                  -0.1201         0.0934             -1.3
       logMb           7.69 - 11.43                   0.0368         0.0294              1.2
         SB0            1.32 - 4.38                   0.0841         0.0371              2.3
  what the emergent-a_0 (LambdaCDM) reading needs: at fixed mass, a_0 tracks halo concentration, which rises for earlier-forming
  galaxies -- bulge-dominated, early-type, gas-poor.  So it predicts a POSITIVE slope with bulge fraction and a NEGATIVE one with
  gas fraction, of order the +0.25 dex per unit concentration the crispy-gap script computed.  The framework predicts zero for all.
  [PASS] 89 the per-galaxy acceleration scale shows no trend with any formation-epoch proxy (all < 3 sigma) -- but the SAME sample can only detect the emergent-halo reading's 0.25 dex trend at 1.7 sigma, so this null is CONSISTENT WITH the framework and NOT YET decisive against the alternative.  Reported as underpowered, against interest   (type: -0.0229 +- 0.0097 (-2.4 sigma); fbul: +0.1340 +- 0.1456 (+0.9 sigma); fgas: -0.1201 +- 0.0934 (-1.3 sigma); an injected 0.25 dex trend would register at only 1.7 sigma)
  [PASS] M89 mutation control (the check that downgraded 89): injecting a 0.25 dex trend with bulge fraction is recovered exactly in the slope, so the estimator is unbiased -- the limitation is the sample's own spread, not the method   (recovered slope offset = 0.2500 against 0.25 injected; N would have to grow ~3x for 3 sigma)
  what would make 89 decisive: the per-galaxy a_0 spread is 0.30 dex here, dominated by distance and inclination errors; the
  distance-free estimator (hunt item 64) cuts that budget to 6.4%, which is the route to a 3-sigma statement on this axis.

